abstract: >-
  We prove that the integers up to N for which the Erdős–Straus equation has
  no solution number at most N exp(-(c1 - eps)(log N)^(2/3)) with an explicit
  constant c1, given by an integral; c1 = 0.6792 numerically and
  c1 >= 0.66571 in closed form. The residue classes from the three corners of
  the triangle of factorisations give exactly c0 = 9/(2^(8/3) pi^(2/3)) =
  0.6607..., the best constant the large sieve gives with them; the gain comes
  from classes of the centre, counted with Maynard's theorem on primes in
  progressions to large moduli. We also find the full asymptotic expansion,
  with a power-saving error term, of the weighted count of search pairs that
  controls a recent sieve bound for the equation; its first two terms were
  found earlier by Liu.
